  "policy_prompt_for_action": [
    {
      "modify_from": "Use closing and early lifting demonstration frames together to identify the segment actually between the fingertips; the prominently projecting segment may be the free end. Trace the grasped segment back to the unobstructed initial peg. Fit its endpoints and color boundary in the current calibrated views and recover the demonstrated contact position along that segment, rather than using the peg midpoint.",
      "modify_to": "Fit the whole peg and the opposing fingertip contacts together across closing and early lifting demonstration frames. Track both colored segments, their boundary, and the contact region back to the unobstructed initial peg. The grasped segment is the one enclosed by the fingers; neither prominence nor visibility in the wrist image establishes whether a segment is held or free. Recover the contact's longitudinal position relative to the segment endpoints and color boundary, then map that position onto the calibrated current peg axis before choosing the approach or closing descent. Do not substitute the peg midpoint, segment midpoint, or reached TCP for this contact."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Fit each pose relative to the entered box face using neighboring demonstration frames, visible peg endpoints, color boundary, and fingertip geometry. Recover the TCP-to-peg offset and constrain the actual held-peg offset with the current wrist image.",
      "modify_to": "Fit each pose relative to the entered box face using neighboring demonstration frames, visible peg edges and endpoints, color boundary, and the midpoint between opposing fingertip contacts. Recover the rigid peg-to-TCP offset and constrain the actual held-peg offset with the current wrist image. For a candidate hand orientation R and a peg landmark with hand-relative offset r, enforce p_TCP = p_landmark - R r; check consistency across several visible landmarks. Use these same contact constraints to reconstruct low staging independently, without rounding coordinates or lowering from the measured transport endpoint."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Resolve yaw's pi ambiguity using the directed vector from the held end to the free end.",
      "modify_to": "Resolve yaw's pi ambiguity using the directed held-to-free-end vector together with the demonstrated peg-to-hand contact relationship; do not assume that the positive hand axis points toward the free tip. Preserve demonstrated non-cardinal orientations."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "If the hand is at low staging, target the next demonstrated opening-entry configuration. Entry may combine inward translation with height and pitch correction rather than remaining horizontal at constant height. Align the free tip with the opening, not the TCP.",
      "modify_to": "If the hand is at completed low staging, target the endpoint of the demonstrated first inward alignment advance, not an arbitrary sampled image along that advance. For the nearly level entry pattern, this advance is approximately 0.05 m along the inward face normal, with demonstrated lateral and height corrections. Compute it from the reconstructed nominal staging pose rather than simply adding displacement to the reached TCP. Entry may instead combine inward translation with substantial height and pitch correction when supported by the demonstration. Align the free tip with the opening, not the TCP, and keep the gripper closed."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "After an inward advance, compare the current motion frames and free-tip-to-opening relationship with the ordered demonstration configurations. Distinguish low staging, intermediate opening entry, and terminal insertion; completing one advance does not automatically select the terminal pose. If the free tip remains outside and a demonstrated intermediate entry configuration remains, target that configuration before terminal insertion. Reconstruct each target's position, height, and orientation from its demonstrated geometry rather than imposing a fixed advance length or a universal 0.05 m cap.",
      "modify_to": "After an inward advance, compare the current motion frames and peg-to-opening relationship with the demonstrated motion segments. Video frames sample motion; they are not a list of commanded waypoints. Identify completed staging, the endpoint of the first inward alignment advance, and terminal insertion from neighboring frames and changes in translation, height, or orientation. Require evidence of a distinct completed intermediate maneuver before selecting another intermediate target; an intervening sampled pose or a still-exposed free tip alone is not such evidence. Once the first alignment advance has completed, target terminal insertion unless a distinct further alignment maneuver is supported. Preserve any such demonstrated maneuver, including tilted-entry corrections, rather than skipping it. Reconstruct target geometry without a universal advance length or 0.05 m cap. Through an otherwise straight insertion advance, preserve the established insertion-axis orientation; change it only when peg-axis and fingertip-contact geometry support the change, not merely because the projected arm or wrist silhouette changes."
    }
  ],
  "refused": "No memory changes are committed: the reports identify visual reconstruction and motion-segmentation errors, not unavailable historical facts. Episode-specific color assignments, coordinates, and video labels are not added as lookup rules. The round-1 clause requiring an intermediate target whenever the free tip remains outside is replaced because this round establishes that it can turn samples of continuous motion into spurious stops. Its protection for genuinely distinct intermediate entry maneuvers is retained. Conversely, the proposed unconditional transition from the first advance to terminal insertion is not committed, because it would discard the earlier evidence for distinct tilted-entry or further alignment maneuvers. Approximate 0.05 m entry applies only to the demonstrated nearly level pattern, not every insertion. Universal level orientation, fixed staging heights, arbitrary transport clearance, and reached-TCP targets remain excluded. No post-success release convention is introduced; neither round supplies a general destination for that continuation."
